u2013B5 melds \u2192 no.  \n  - **All Chows** is possible if your last meld is a chow and W1 is pair, but one meld is pung (T6) \u2192 no.  \n\n**Wait possibilities:**  \nYour hand now needs one meld and a pair.  \nIf you discard F3, your hand is all W suit except F3 gone, so you could aim for a W-based chow (W4\u2013W5\u2013W6 etc.) or pair W7 with another W7.  \nIf you discard W4 or W7, you keep F3 which could become a pair if drawn again.  \n\n**Available tiles (unshown counts):**  \n- W4: 4 left, W7: 1 left, W1: 2 left, F3: 2 left (since you have 1, 3 total minus 1 yours, maybe 2 left).  \n- W2: 0 left (dead), W8: 0 left, W9: 0 left \u2192 W chows limited. W chows possible: W3\u2013W4\u2013W5 (W3 gone in kong), W4\u2013W5\u2013W6 (possible), W6\u2013W7\u2013W8 (impossible, W8=0), W1\u2013W2\u2013W3 (impossible).  \n\nGiven the dead tiles (W2, W8, W9), the best shot for a meld in W suit is:  \n1. W4\u2013W5\u2013W6 chow (need W5, W6). Both have 4 left each, good probability.  \n2. W7 pair + something else? W7 only 1 left, so unlikely to pair.  \n3. F3 pair: 2 left, possible.  \n\nSo ideal plan:  \nKeep W1 (pair candidate) + W4 (for chow with W5,W6) + maybe W7? But W7 is a terminal, may be hard to use.  \n\n**Immediate discard choice:**  \nF3: discard honor, reduces honors (helps No Honors pattern possible later), but F3 pair chance lost.  \nW7: discard terminal, keeps F3 possibly for pair, but W7 could pair with 1 left.  \nW1: discard a pair candidate, but you have 2, discarding one leaves only 1 W1, so pair gone.  \nW4: discard middle tile useful for chow W4\u2013W5\u2013W6.  \n\nBest is to keep W1 pair, keep W4 for chow chance, discard less useful tile:  \n- W7: terminal, no chow potential with W8 dead.  \n- F3: honor, maybe pair possible but if you aim for No Honors, discard it.  \n\nGiven scoring patterns:  \nIf you win with W1 pair + W4\u2013W5\u2013W6 chow + existing melds, you\u2019d have:  \n- No Honors (if F3 gone) = 1 pt.  \n- One Voided Suit? Still have T and B in melds \u2192 no.  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors? T6 pung not terminal/honor \u2192 no.  \n- All Simples? T6 pung is simple, W3 kong simple, B3\u2013B5 chow simple, W1 pair terminal \u2192 All Simples not satisfied.  \n\nBut to maximize points, having a pair of honors (F3) gives Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 pt) if you pair it, but to meld it you\u2019d need three total. Hard.  \n\nSafer: discard F3 to aim for No Honors (1 pt) + possibly others.  \n\n**Also consider:** Prevailing wind and seat wind are North (F4), not F3, so F3 gives no extra wind points.  \n\nSo discard **F3** now.  \n\n---\n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding F3 removes an honor, keeping the hand simpler (potential No Honors pattern) and preserves W1 pair and W4 for possible chow with W5/W6, both of which have 4 tiles left in the wall. W7 is terminal but only 1 left, so pairing it is unlikely, but it could be used in a chow with W5\u2013W6\u2013W7 if W7 is kept and W5/W6 drawn \u2014 but W7 as 7 needs W6 (4 left) and W5 (4 left), possible but less flexible than W4\u2013W5\u2013W6 chow. W4 is more central. Thus F3 is least useful for building a winning hand given tile availability and scoring potential.  \n\n---\n\n**Answer:** Play F3"}
